,"index_state":"retained_generated_snapshot"},"guide":{"availability":"available","guide_id":"paper_module.target_shape_tactic_routing","plain":"Different kinds of statement call for different automatic proof methods, called tactics, so a program must choose among them. This component is given notes on which tactics are available and example cases giving a statement's shape, the candidates and the tactic chosen. It checks each choice against availability and the preferences for that shape, and requires a fallback when the shape is unknown or the preferred tactic is unavailable. It writes a routing report for use before a proof attempt. The small step that picks the first usable preferred tactic is checked by a mathematical proof against written rules.","plain_availability":"available","ref":"paper_modules/target_shape_tactic_routing.md","sha256":"a78eef7c7417ba099c134185cb2abf17882f57b6fb16734347c714e4e21d6388","text":"# Choose tactics for a target shape\n\n> **In plain terms.** Different kinds of statement call for different automatic proof methods, called tactics, so a program must choose among them. This component is given notes on which tactics are available and example cases giving a statement's shape, the candidates and the tactic chosen. It checks each choice against availability and the preferences for that shape, and requires a fallback when the shape is unknown or the preferred tactic is unavailable. It writes a routing report for use before a proof attempt. The small step that picks the first usable preferred tactic is checked by a mathematical proof against written rules.\n\nThis gate reads recorded tactic availability and target-shape route cases, then checks the selected tactic against the candidates, availability and shape-specific preferences. It writes a routing report for use before proof execution.\n\n## Run the example\n\nFrom the repository root, with Python 3.11 or newer available:\n\n```bash\nPYTHONPATH=src python3 -m microcosm_core.organs.target_shape_tactic_routing_gate run \\\n  --input fixtures/first_wave/target_shape_tactic_routing_gate/input \\\n  --out .microcosm/component-guides/target_shape_tactic_routing_gate \\\n  --acceptance-out .microcosm/component-guides/target_shape_tactic_routing_gate/acceptance.json\n```\n\nThe command writes local output under `.microcosm/component-guides/`. Check the returned status and the result receipt; a blocked result reports findings for the supplied inputs.\n\n## What this checks\n\nFor example, the test for a Boolean double-negation goal checks that the preferred available tactic is `decide`. The gate rejects selecting an unavailable tactic and requires a fallback for an unknown shape or an unavailable preferred tactic. The input includes invalid post-execution and proof-body cases.\n\n## Limits\n\nChecks each tactic choice against recorded availability and target-shape preferences, rejecting an unavailable tactic and requiring a fallback for an unknown shape.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe selector calls the [proved tactic-selection core](../formal/components/tactic_selection/README.md). Given ordered preferences and candidates marked known, available and allowed, it selects the first eligible preferred tactic, otherwise the first eligible candidate, or reports no selection. The proof covers that ordering for finite inputs; Python supplies the preferences and availability observations.\n\nThe example uses that core. `make component-replays` separately applies the shared [replay admission check](../formal/replay/README.md) to the exported bundle.\n\n## Source\n\n[Implementation](../src/microcosm_core/organs/target_shape_tactic_routing_gate.py) · [Example input](../fixtures/first_wave/target_shape_tactic_routing_gate/input/) · [Tests](../tests/test_target_shape_tactic_routing_gate.py) · [Historical sample result](../receipts/first_wave/target_shape_tactic_routing_gate/target_shape_tactic_routing_result.json) · [JSON record](target_shape_tactic_routing.json)\n\nBackground: [Lean tactics](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/Tactics/).\n"},"proofs":[{"claim_ids":["claim::target_shape_tactic_routing_gate"],"component_ids":["target_shape_tactic_routing_gate"],"current_backend_binding":{"status":"not_checked"},"fresh_prover":{"status":"not_recorded_by_index"},"guide":{"availability":"available","plain":null,"plain_availability":"unrecorded","ref":"formal/components/tactic_selection/README.md","sha256":"34c2af590818c5d68bccab01c674db21a1a0b9607813621f6f6a6ea09abef8a1","text":"# Tactic selection decision core\n\n`Select` receives an ordered preference list and ordered candidate observations. Each candidate carries a normalized identity and booleans saying whether it is known, available and allowed. It selects the first preference with an eligible candidate, or the first eligible candidate when no preference matches, or reports no selection. The postconditions state eligibility, first-preference precedence, first-candidate tie order and the no-candidate case for arbitrary finite inputs.\n\nThe component's Python code supplies the preferences, candidate order and membership observations. Python normalization, source availability records and the runtime boundary remain outside the proof; a chosen tactic is not a proof that it will solve a goal. The generated Python is called through the component worker, with no Python decision fallback. Dafny, its solver and compiler, the worker and Python remain trusted. The source-linked mutant drops the known-candidate condition; the original specification must reject it.\n"},"index_state":"retained_generated_snapshot","modeled_contracts":[{"assumptions":"Trusted host supplies normalized observations; adapter, isolated worker, Dafny, compiler and runtime remain trusted.","contract_sha256":"e5a8dd187b781285250b0f8f68c0a42eb32de1cd09d55f29f3614cabebaec18a","descriptor_origin":"metadata_contract","entry_theorem_refs":["formal/components/tactic_selection/TacticSelection.dfy#Select"],"input_mod